<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="ch-servos">
    <title>Servomotors</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            The goal of this chapter is to control the position of a servomotor from
            the STM32C031C6.  A servomotor is a motor that goes to the angle we ask for
            and holds it there, rather than turning continuously.  Inside it are a DC
            motor, a gear train, a potentiometer and a control circuit, and all we
            supply as a command is one signal: a pulse whose width carries the
            position we want.  To produce that pulse we'll use
            TIM14's PWM (pulse-width modulation) output again, as we did for the motor,
            but this time the period is about 20 ms rather than the fast switching a
            motor's speed needed.  We'll read the position we want from a potentiometer
            with the ADC (analog-to-digital converter), as we did in the ADC chapter.
        </p>
        <p>
            This chapter covers two class days.  On the first we'll design the timer
            for the servo's pulse and check on the AD2 oscilloscope that the pulse is
            the one we designed.  On the
            second we'll connect the servo to its own 5 V supply and watch it follow the
            potentiometer.  In Lab 8 this servo carries the arm of a solar tracker.
            <xref ref="sec-servo-reference"/> collects the pulse convention, the timer
            arithmetic and the driver for looking up later.
        </p>
        <objectives>
            <ul>
                <li>Explain what is inside a servomotor and how its built-in feedback loop takes the shaft to a commanded position and holds it there.</li>
                <li>State how a hobby servo is commanded, and say how that differs from the way PWM set a motor's speed.</li>
                <li>Design TIM14 for a 50 Hz pulse: choose a prescaler and an auto-reload value that fit the 16-bit register, and judge the resulting resolution against what the servo can use.</li>
                <li>Compute the compare values for 1 ms, 1.5 ms and 2 ms pulses, and map a 12-bit ADC reading onto that range in integer arithmetic.</li>
                <li>Build and verify, on the AD2 oscilloscope, a potentiometer-controlled pulse train on PA7 (D11).</li>
                <li>Wire and power a servo from the regulator board, and say why it is never powered from the Nucleo.</li>
            </ul>
        </objectives>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-servo-before-class">
        <title>Before Class: What a Servomotor Is and How It Is Commanded</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                In the motor chapter we set a DC motor's direction and its speed.  A
                <term>servomotor</term> does a different job: we tell it an angle, and
                its own electronics take the shaft there and hold it.  This reading covers what is
                inside a servomotor, what it does, how we command it with a pulse, and
                where its power comes from.  In class we'll design that pulse and
                produce it.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-servo-inside">
            <title>What Is Inside a Servomotor</title>
            <p>
                <xref ref="fig-servo-cutaway"/> shows what is inside a hobby servomotor like the one we'll be using.  There
                is a DC motor that is connected to a little circuit board, which
                includes an H-bridge (the four-switch circuit from the motor chapter
                that can drive a motor in either direction).  The motor shaft goes
                through a gear train that gears it way down to the output shaft, which
                is not turning very fast.  A potentiometer is attached to that output
                shaft, and it gives you the angular position of the shaft.
                Corresponding to that angular position the potentiometer puts out a
                voltage, and that voltage is fed into the control circuit, which then
                adjusts the motor's speed and direction through the H-bridge to align
                with the angular position that is currently being commanded of the
                servo.
            </p>
            <p>
                Why all the gears?  Power is the product of torque and angular velocity.
                Through the gear train the speed going in is much higher than the speed
                going out.  Since power is (except for friction) preserved,
                <m>\tau_\text{in}\, \omega_\text{in} \approx \tau_\text{out}\, \omega_\text{out}</m>,
                the torque going out is much higher than the torque going in.  In goes a
                high speed motor with not a lot of torque, and out comes a low speed with
                a large amount of torque.
            </p>
            <!-- P-15: the picture's labels (high speed / low torque in, 60 rpm / high
                 torque out) are the GIVEN of Part 1's commit, which asks where the
                 extra torque comes from.  The answer (power is conserved through the
                 gear train) is not in this figure or in this reading on purpose. -->
            <figure xml:id="fig-servo-cutaway">
                <caption>Inside a hobby servomotor: the DC motor, the gear train
                (labeled gearbox), the potentiometer on the output shaft, and the
                control circuit with its integrated H-bridge.  The horn on top, the
                plastic piece that bolts onto the output shaft, is what a load is
                attached to.  The picture also gives what goes into the gear train and
                what comes out of it: high speed and low torque at the motor, 60 rpm and
                high torque at the output shaft.  Image from
                <url href="https://howtomechatronics.com/how-it-works/how-servo-motors-work-how-to-control-servos-using-arduino/">howtomechatronics.com</url>.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day15-Servos/slide06_d84321e4.png" width="75%"/>
            </figure>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-servo-holds">
            <title>What a Servomotor Does</title>
            <p>
                A servomotor provides precise position control, and it does this by
                feedback (<xref ref="fig-servo-feedback"/>).  The command position input
                is a voltage.  The potentiometer, turned by the motor, outputs a voltage
                proportional to the position of the output shaft.  The controller
                compares the two voltages, and their difference is the position error.
                The controller drives the motor until the position error is zero.  How
                exactly the controller does this is something you can study in ENGS 26.
                Our servomotors have the controller built in; we'll look below at the
                signal that carries the command position into it.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-servo-feedback">
                <caption>How a servomotor works: the feedback loop inside it.  The
                command position enters at the left, and the potentiometer's
                measurement of the output position is subtracted from it.  The
                difference is the error.  The controller drives the motor, the motor
                turns the gears, and the gears turn both the output shaft and the
                potentiometer, which closes the loop.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day15-Servos/slide08_095020b6.png" width="95%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                It is the ability to go to precisely some place that is the really
                valuable feature, and servomotors are used wherever that is needed:
                robot joints, aircraft control surfaces (both hobby and full scale),
                camera auto-focus, conveyor belts, solar trackers, computer-numerical-control (CNC) tools for metal and wood, antenna positioning in azimuth and elevation, and opening and
                closing doors, vents and valves.  In Lab 8 you will build a solar
                tracker.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-servo-ours">
            <title>Our Servomotor and Its Datasheet</title>
            <p>
                Our servomotor is the Tower Pro SG92R micro servo
                (<xref ref="fig-servo-sg92r"/>).  Its
                <url href="external/datasheets/C17481_SG92R_datasheet.pdf">datasheet</url>
                is one page, so look through the whole of it.  The relevant specs are the
                stall torque, 2.5 kgf-cm at 4.8 V, which is the torque the servo puts out
                if you try to resist its turning; the operating speed, 0.1 s per 60°; the
                operating voltage, 4.8 V; the gear material, POM (polyoxymethylene, a hard
                plastic) with a carbon-fiber gear; and a dead band width of 1 µs, which
                we'll come back to once we talk about communicating with the servo.  A kgf
                (kilogram-force) is the gravity exerted on 1 kg of mass, so kgf-cm, which
                the sheet writes as kg/cm, is still force times distance.  Please do not
                try to force a servo's horn around by hand.
            </p>
            <!-- Question 1b (ground truth §9) is still open: no sheet carries a current
                 figure.  "about 250 mA while moving, and much more when it is stalled" is
                 Petra's slide 28 number, used in the reading, rq-servo-supply, Part 6 and
                 the Reference; all four change together if she prefers none. -->
            <figure xml:id="fig-servo-sg92r">
                <caption>The Tower Pro SG92R micro servo, with its three-lead cable: brown,
                red and orange.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day15-Servos/towerPro.jpg" width="55%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                The SG92R is the newer version of the SG90: according to Tower Pro, its
                POM and carbon-fiber gears and control arms are more durable than the
                SG90's nylon gears, and it has more torque.  The
                <url href="external/datasheets/Servosg90_datasheet.pdf">SG90 datasheet</url>
                carries more information, all of it still relevant to the updated model:
                the operating range of 4.8 to 6 V, which is why the regulator board's 5 V
                suits it, a drawing of the three leads, and a drawing of the command
                pulse.  We'll refer to both drawings below.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-servo-command">
            <title>How a Servomotor Is Commanded</title>
            <p>
                In order to control the position of a servomotor, we first need to
                understand the protocol we use to communicate to the servo the position
                we want it to move to.  The servo expects a pulse on its signal lead,
                between 1 ms and 2 ms wide, repeated regularly, roughly every 20 ms (so at
                50 Hz).  The width of the pulse is conveying information to the
                controller on how far to turn the motor (<xref ref="fig-servo-pulses"/>):
                the servo's controller electronics convert the pulse width into the
                control voltage that its feedback loop works from.  A pulse train like
                this is a PWM (pulse-width modulation) signal, so we can produce it with
                the same timer we used for the motor, but it is a different use of PWM.
                For the motor, the fraction of each period spent HIGH set an average
                voltage; here the width of the pulse itself is the message, and the
                period only has to be roughly right.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-servo-pulses">
                <caption>The command pulse.  Each row is one pulse width, 1 ms, 1.5 ms
                and 2 ms, repeated about every 20 ms.  The angles and the turn directions are the convention described below, and
                the drawing is not to scale.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day15-Servos/fig-servo-pulses.png" width="95%"/>
            </figure>
            <!-- Question 6 (ground truth §9) is still open: what students actually see
                 at 1 ms and 2 ms.  The paragraph below states the convention and her
                 "don't go all that far" caveat, which is true either way. -->
            <p>
                Servos do not have an I2C interface.  The technology is fairly old, and
                this pulse is the historic way to control them.  By convention, a
                1.5 ms pulse brings the servo to the middle, a zero angle; 1 ms turns it
                to −90 degrees and 2 ms turns it to +90 degrees.  Those endpoints are
                hypothetical: the SG90 datasheet says the servo rotates approximately
                180 degrees, but in reality our servos do not go that far, and how far
                depends on the individual servo.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-servo-power">
            <title>Where the Servo's Power Comes From</title>
            <p>
                The servo runs on 4.8 to 6 V and draws about 250 mA while moving, and much
                more if it is stalled.  Because of that current draw, the servo is powered from a separate supply and never from
                the Nucleo's 3.3 V or 5 V pin.  We use the same regulator board as we
                did for the motor (<xref ref="fig-tb6612-regulator"/>): the servo's
                power lead goes to the board's 5V pin, and the board and the Nucleo share
                a ground, because the pulse from PA7 is a voltage measured against the
                Nucleo's 0 V and without a shared ground the servo has nothing to measure
                it against.  Only the signal lead goes to the STM32C031C6, at PA7 (D11).
            </p>
            <p>
                The servo has three leads.  The SG90 datasheet's wiring drawing colors
                them brown for ground, red for power and orange for the signal, and those
                are the colors on the servo in your kit.  In general, on a hobby servo the
                center lead is power, the darker of the two outer leads is ground, and
                the remaining lead is the signal.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <reading-questions xml:id="rq-servo">
            <exercise xml:id="rq-servo-width">
                <statement>
                    <p>The servo's controller reads a position command out of the pulse
                    train we send it.  Which property of that signal carries the
                    command?</p>
                </statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>The width of the HIGH pulse, between 1 ms and 2 ms.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  The controller measures how long the
                        signal stays HIGH in each period and converts that width into
                        its command voltage.  The width is the command, and the pulses
                        have to keep coming.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The duty cycle, the fraction of each period the signal is HIGH.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>That is what set the motor's speed in the motor
                        chapter.  A 1 ms pulse every 20 ms is a 5% duty cycle, but the
                        servo reads the 1 ms, not the 5%: at a different period the same
                        duty cycle would be a different width and a different
                        position.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The frequency of the pulses.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The frequency is fixed at about 50 Hz and is the
                        same for every position.  It carries no command.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The voltage level of the pulse.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The pulse is a logic level, 3.3 V from our pin,
                        and it is the same for every position.  The command is in the
                        pulse's width.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-servo-leads">
                <statement>
                    <p>The SG90 datasheet's wiring drawing shows the servo's three
                    leads.  Which lead is which?</p>
                </statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>The center lead is power, the brown lead is ground, and the orange lead is the signal.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  The center lead is power on every hobby
                        servo, the darker outer lead is ground, and the other outer
                        lead carries the pulse.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The center lead is the signal, and the two outer leads are power and ground.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The center lead is power.  Wiring the pulse pin
                        to it puts a 3.3 V logic output on the servo's supply rail, and
                        ties the 5 V supply to the signal lead instead.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The red lead is the signal, because red marks the pin the microcontroller drives.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Red is power on the datasheet's drawing, as it is on most hobby servos.  The signal lead is orange.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The brown lead is power, because it is the darkest.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The darker outer lead is ground, not power.  Power
                        is the center lead.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-servo-supply">
                <statement>
                    <p>Why is the servo's power lead connected to the regulator board's
                    5V pin rather than to a pin on the Nucleo?</p>
                </statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>The servo draws about 250 mA while moving and much more when stalled, which is more than the Nucleo's supply should be asked for.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  The regulator board supplies the servo's
                        current from the wall adapter, and only the signal lead goes to
                        the STM32C031C6.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The servo needs a 5 V signal on its pulse lead, and the Nucleo's pins are 3.3 V.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The pulse from PA7 is 3.3 V and the servo reads it
                        fine.  The reason is the current on the power lead, not the
                        voltage on the signal lead.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>Powered from 3.3 V the servo would turn in the wrong direction.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The direction the servo turns is decided by its
                        controller from the pulse width.  The supply voltage sets how
                        much current the servo can draw and how hard it can push, not
                        which way.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The Nucleo cannot produce a 50 Hz signal.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>It can, and TIM14 will produce it.  The signal
                        comes from the Nucleo; only the servo's power comes from the
                        regulator board.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-servo-speed">
                <statement>
                    <p>The SG92R datasheet gives the operating speed as 0.1 s per 60°
                    at 4.8 V.  About how long does the servo take to swing from one end
                    of its travel to the other, its nominal 180°?</p>
                </statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>About 0.3 s.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  180° is three times 60°, so three times
                        0.1 s.  This is the number that matters when a program has to wait for the arm to
                        arrive before it takes its next reading.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>About 0.1 s.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>0.1 s is the time for 60°, a third of the
                        travel.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>About 3 s.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>That is ten times too long.  The datasheet's
                        0.1 s per 60° gives 0.3 s for 180°.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>It depends on the pulse width we send.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The pulse width sets where the arm goes, not how
                        fast it gets there.  The speed is a property of the servo,
                        and the datasheet gives it.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-servo-error">
                <statement>
                    <p>In the feedback diagram in <xref ref="fig-servo-feedback"/>, the
                    error is a difference.  A difference of what two quantities?</p>
                </statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>The commanded position and the potentiometer's measurement of the output position.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  Both are voltages inside the servo, and
                        the controller drives the motor until they are
                        equal.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The pulse width and the pulse period.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Those describe the command signal we send in.
                        The error is formed inside the servo, between the command and
                        the measured position.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The motor's speed and its torque.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Speed and torque are what the motor produces on
                        the way to the position.  The error is about position, not
                        about either of them.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The supply voltage and the motor's back-EMF.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>That difference sets the current through a DC
                        motor, from Day 11.  The servo's error compares positions,
                        not voltages across the motor.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>
        </reading-questions>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- Day 15 In-Class.  Tuesday, 110 min (plans/day15.md, Gate 1 applied).
         Budget, for the deck (delivery 2): settle 3, announcements 2, Part 1 = 6 (2+4; the gears
         reveal moved to the reading at Petra's pass 1), Part 2 = 8 (5+3), Part 3 = 18
         (2+5+7+4), Part 4 = 10 (3+1+2+4), Part 5 = 51 (5+8+4+5+24+5, the checkpoint
         falling inside the 24-minute build at about minute 78), Part 6 = 7 (4+3),
         close 5.  Total 110.
         OWES (delivery 2): refPage recall slides for subsec-day11-actuator-chain
         (Part 1), fig-tim14-block-full (Part 3), subsec-day11x-driver and
         fig-tim14-register-map (Part 4).
         Cut order if a part overruns: Part 6 to its first beat, then Part 1's
         push-the-arm beat to one sentence, then Part 4's TIM16 beat to "read it
         tonight".  Never cut Part 3 or Part 5's verification. -->
    <section xml:id="sec-servo-day15">
        <title>Day 15 In-Class: Commanding a Servomotor</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Today we'll design the signal that commands a servomotor, and we'll
                produce it on the STM32C031C6.  We'll start with what is inside the
                servo and what its feedback loop does, then work out how TIM14 has to be
                set up for a 50 Hz pulse, and then build a program that turns the
                potentiometer's position into a pulse width and check that pulse on the
                AD2 oscilloscope.  At the end we'll look at how the servo is powered; we'll
                wire it up tomorrow.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <!-- Part 1: 6 min = 2 (recall) + 4 (push commit and reveal) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day15-loop">
            <title>Part 1: A Motor with a Loop Inside It</title>
            <p>
                Inside the servo is a DC permanent-magnet motor, the same motor we
                studied in the motor chapter (<xref ref="subsec-day11-actuator-chain"/>),
                and one fact from that chapter is the key fact we need going forward:
                the torque a motor produces is proportional to the current through it.
                Now we'll add on to it.
            </p>
            <p>
                The gear train trades the motor's speed for torque at the output shaft,
                and the potentiometer on that shaft is what closes the loop
                (<xref ref="fig-servo-feedback"/>): its voltage is compared with the
                command voltage, and the controller drives the motor until the difference
                is zero.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-day15-push">
                <title>What if something pushed on the horn?</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>Suppose the servo has reached its commanded position and is
                    holding it, and something pushes on the horn.  What does the servo
                    do, and what inside it makes that happen?  (Think it through; do not
                    try it on your servo.)</p>
                </statement>
            </activity>
            <p>
                Once in its position, if something turns the horn, the potentiometer is
                going to sense that, and the controller is going to counteract that
                error.  So the servo will strongly resist.  That is also why the position
                can be measured with a potentiometer, which only turns part of a
                revolution, and why you never turn a servo by hand or take it all the way
                around: if you do, you break it.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 2: 8 min = 5 (between-pulses commit, reveal, dead band) + 3 (Table 12) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day15-command">
            <title>Part 2: How We Command the Servo</title>
            <p>
                The signal that commands the servo's position is a pulse, and today we'll
                design that pulse and produce it.  It is the pulse from the reading (<xref ref="fig-servo-pulses"/>):
                1 ms to 2 ms wide, repeated every 20 ms.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-day15-between-pulses">
                <title>Between the pulses</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>The pulse is at most 2 ms wide and the period is 20 ms, so for
                    most of every period the signal is LOW.  What does the servo do
                    between two pulses?  And what happens if the pulses stop
                    coming?</p>
                </statement>
            </activity>
            <p>
                As long as the same pulse keeps arriving at the servo, it stays at that one position and is held there.  If you shut the signal down there is no
                power on the motor.  So a servo spins to the correct position and then
                sticks there as long as you apply the same pulses.  The signal is a
                position signal, and you have to keep sending it.  That makes this a
                different use of PWM from the motor's: there the duty cycle set an
                average voltage that went to the motor, and here we are using PWM as a
                way to communicate with the controller rather than sending it to the
                motor directly.
            </p>
            <p>
                The controller also has a limit to what it can resolve.  The SG92R
                datasheet lists a <term>dead band width</term> of 1 µs: a change in
                pulse width smaller than that produces no change in the commanded position.  We'll need that number when we choose how to configure our timer to produce the pulses.
            </p>
            <p>
                Almost every pin on the Nucleo is capable of PWM, driven by one timer or
                another (<xref ref="fig-servo-pwm-pins"/>).  We look up which timer drives a given pin, and which alternate function to
                select, in the datasheet.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-servo-pwm-pins">
                <caption>The Nucleo-C031C6's headers, with each pin marked by what it
                can do.  Almost every pin can carry a PWM output, driven by one timer or
                another; Table 12 of the STM32C031C6 datasheet, <em>Pin assignment and
                description</em>, says which timer and which alternate function for
                each pin.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day15-Servos/slide14_ef97f387.png" width="90%"/>
            </figure>
            <activity xml:id="act-day15-tim14-pins">
                <title>Which pins can TIM14 drive?</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>Two documents describe what is in front of you.  The
                    <url href="external/stm32c031_datasheet.pdf">STM32C031C6 datasheet</url>
                    describes the chip, and which timer a pin can carry, and under which
                    alternate function, is a property of the chip: Table 12, <em>Pin
                    assignment and description</em>, on page 30 names the functions, and
                    the alternate-function tables on pages 36 to 39 give the AF number.
                    Which chip pin sits at which Arduino header position is a property of
                    the board, and that is the Nucleo's pinout
                    (<xref ref="fig-servo-pwm-pins"/>).</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day15-pins-find">
                    <statement>
                        <p>Fill in the table: every pin that can carry TIM14_CH1, the
                        alternate function it is under, and, if the pin reaches the
                        Arduino header, its name there.</p>
                        <tabular halign="center" top="major" bottom="major" left="minor" right="minor">
                            <col width="25%"/>
                            <col width="25%"/>
                            <col width="50%"/>
                            <row bottom="major">
                                <cell>Pin</cell>
                                <cell>AF</cell>
                                <cell>Arduino header name, if any</cell>
                            </row>
                            <row bottom="minor"><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
                            <row bottom="minor"><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
                            <row bottom="minor"><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
                            <row bottom="minor"><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
                            <row><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
                        </tabular>
                    </statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day15-pins-choose">
                    <statement><p>One of them carried the motor's PWM in the motor
                    chapter.  Which is it, what is its name on the Arduino header, and
                    what does choosing it save us today?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day15-tim14-pins">
                <p>Table 12 lists TIM14_CH1 on five pins: PF0, PA4, PA7, PB1 and PA8.  The
                alternate-function tables give the number for each: PA4 and PA7 are AF4,
                PB1 is AF0, PA8 is AF13, and PF0 is AF2.  Neither PA8 nor PF0 reaches the Arduino header (PF0 is an oscillator pin), so the real choice is PA4 (A2), PA7 (D11) and PB1 (A3). PA7 is D11 on the Arduino header, and it is where the motor's PWM
                was, so the pin configuration in the driver, alternate-function mode and
                AF4, is exactly the one we read on Day 11x and nothing about the pin setup
                changes today.  PA4 and PB1 are also A2 and A3 on the analog header, which
                is another reason to leave them alone.</p>
            </instructor>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 3: 18 min = 2 (callback and 16-bit recall) + 5 (commit 1) + 7 (commit 2) + 4 (commit 3) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day15-timer-design">
            <title>Part 3: Designing the Timer for the Servo's Pulse</title>
            <p>
                On Day 11x we worked out what the prescaler and the auto-reload value would
                have to be for a 50 Hz PWM (<xref ref="task-day11x-stretch-50hz"/>): the
                12 MHz clock has to be divided down by a factor of 240,000 to get 50 Hz
                (12,000,000 clock cycles per second divided by 50 periods per second is
                240,000 cycles per period), and the prescaler and the counter split that
                factor between them.  Today we'll run that search again, for the servo, with a new
                constraint: the servo only uses pulse widths between 1 ms and 2 ms, and
                its dead band sets the finest change in width it can use.  The numbers we settle here become the five <c>#define</c> lines of the program we'll build afterwards.
            </p>
            <p>
                Recall how TIM14 makes the pulse (<xref ref="fig-tim14-block-full"/>):
                the prescaler divides the 12 MHz clock down, the counter counts the divided pulses up to the auto-reload value ARR and
                starts again.  ARR is a 16-bit register, so the largest count it can hold is 65,535.
            </p>
            <p>
                We need a 50 Hz PWM frequency, so one period is 1/(50 Hz) = 20 ms, and
                the counter needs to take 20 ms to count from 0 to its auto-reload value.
                The counter is clocked from the 12 MHz system clock through the
                prescaler, so there are lots of ways to accomplish this.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-day15-prescaler-table">
                <title>Which of these are usable?</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>Fill in the table on your own.  For each prescaler, what does the counter's clock frequency become, and how many counts does it take to make 20 ms?  Then decide which rows are usable, and why the others are
                    not.</p>
                    <tabular halign="center" top="major" bottom="major" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <col width="30%"/>
                        <col width="35%"/>
                        <col width="35%"/>
                        <row bottom="major">
                            <cell>Prescaler</cell>
                            <cell>Timer clock</cell>
                            <cell>Counts for 20 ms (must fit)</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row bottom="minor"><cell>1</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
                        <row bottom="minor"><cell>2</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
                        <row bottom="minor"><cell>6</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
                        <row bottom="minor"><cell>60</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
                        <row><cell>240</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
                    </tabular>
                </statement>
            </activity>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day15-prescaler-table">
                <p>Down the rows: prescaler 1 gives a 12 MHz timer clock and 240,000
                counts for 20 ms; 2 gives 6 MHz and 120,000; 6 gives 2 MHz and 40,000;
                60 gives 200 kHz and 4,000; 240 gives 50 kHz and 1,000.  The first two
                rows are unusable: 240,000 and 120,000 do not fit in a 16-bit
                auto-reload register, whose largest value is 65,535.  The other three
                fit, and the next activity chooses between them.</p>
            </instructor>
            <p>
                A 50 Hz frequency gives a pulse period of <m>T_p = 1/(50\text{ Hz}) =
                20\text{ ms}</m>, and the counter divides that period into ARR + 1 bins,
                where ARR is the auto-reload value the counter counts up to
                (<xref ref="fig-servo-pulse-resolution"/>).  So 20 ms/(ARR + 1)
                is the smallest pulse width the timer can produce, <m>T_0</m>, and it
                is also the smallest change in pulse width it can make.  All useful
                angles occur between a pulse width of 1 ms and 2 ms, so the number of
                steps between 1 ms and 2 ms is the resolution of your servo, and 180°
                divided by that number of steps, using the ±90° convention from the
                reading, is the nominal angle per step.  Putting a number in: on the table's
                first row the period is divided into 240,000 bins, so <m>T_0</m> would be
                20 ms/240,000, about 83 ns.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-servo-pulse-resolution">
                <caption>The pulse period <m>T_p</m> and the smallest pulse width
                <m>T_0</m>.  The counter divides each period into ARR + 1 bins, so
                <m>T_0 = T_p/(\text{ARR}+1)</m> is both the narrowest pulse the timer can
                make and the step between one pulse width and the next.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day15-Servos/fig-servo-pulse-resolution.svg" width="95%"/>
            </figure>
            <activity xml:id="act-day15-resolution-table">
                <title>Which one do you like best?</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>For the three usable rows, fill in the smallest pulse width, the
                    number of steps between 1 ms and 2 ms, and the angle per step.
                    Then choose a row and write down why.</p>
                    <tabular halign="center" top="major" bottom="major" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <col width="16%"/>
                        <col width="16%"/>
                        <col width="24%"/>
                        <col width="22%"/>
                        <col width="22%"/>
                        <row bottom="major">
                            <cell>Prescaler</cell>
                            <cell>ARR + 1</cell>
                            <cell><m>T_0 = 20\text{ ms}/(\text{ARR}+1)</m></cell>
                            <cell>Steps, 1 ms to 2 ms</cell>
                            <cell>Angle per step</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row bottom="minor"><cell>6</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
                        <row bottom="minor"><cell>60</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
                        <row><cell>240</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
                    </tabular>
                </statement>
            </activity>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day15-resolution-table">
                <p>Lead with the chosen row.  A prescaler of 60 gives a 200 kHz timer
                clock and ARR + 1 = 4000, so <m>T_0</m> = 20 ms/4000 = 5 µs; the 1000 µs
                of pulse range between 1 ms and 2 ms is 1000/5 = 200 steps; and 180°/200
                = 0.9° per step.  That is the one.</p>
                <p>Then we'll look at the two we did not choose.  A prescaler of 6 gives
                ARR + 1 = 40,000, <m>T_0</m> = 0.5 µs, 2000 steps and 0.09° per step.
                Amazing angular resolution!  But this thing is made out of plastic.  So, is
                that worth it?  Each step is half the servo's 1 µs dead band, so the servo
                cannot resolve a single step and the controller acts on every second one at
                best, and a plastic gear train has backlash well beyond what 0.09° would
                buy.  That is more finely grained than is worth doing.  A prescaler of 240
                gives ARR + 1 = 1000, <m>T_0</m> = 20 µs, 50 steps and 3.6° per step, four
                times coarser than the row we chose.  So a prescaler of 60 is great, and
                240 would be fine too.</p>
            </instructor>
            <p>
                Timer 14 will count continuously from 0 to the value stored in
                <c>TIM14_ARR</c>.  It will drive the connected pin HIGH while the counter is
                below the value stored in <c>TIM14_CCR1</c>, and drive the pin LOW while
                the counter is between <c>TIM14_CCR1</c> and <c>TIM14_ARR</c>.  Going from
                a pulse width to a count is a division: on the 240 row, where one count is
                20 µs, a 1 ms pulse is 1000 µs ÷ 20 µs = 50 counts.  Now that you have chosen a row, you can work out the numbers the program needs.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-day15-fill-in">
                <title>Choosing TIM14_ARR and TIM14_CCR1</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>Write all of these down before we compare.  The names in capitals are
                    the <c>#define</c> lines of the program we'll build next.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day15-fill-clock">
                    <statement>
                        <p>Fill in the numbers.</p>
                        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="major" left="minor" right="minor">
                            <col width="60%"/>
                            <col width="40%"/>
                            <row bottom="minor"><cell>The STM32C031C6's system clock runs at</cell><cell>______ MHz</cell></row>
                            <row bottom="minor"><cell>The prescaler you chose, <c>PWM_PSC_FACTOR</c></cell><cell></cell></row>
                            <row bottom="minor"><cell>The counter's clock frequency after the prescaler</cell><cell></cell></row>
                            <row bottom="minor"><cell>ARR + 1, the counts in one 20 ms period, <c>PWM_TIMER_MAX</c></cell><cell></cell></row>
                            <row bottom="minor"><cell>CCR1 for a 1 ms pulse, <c>SERVO_MIN</c></cell><cell></cell></row>
                            <row bottom="minor"><cell>CCR1 for a 1.5 ms pulse, <c>SERVO_MID</c></cell><cell></cell></row>
                            <row><cell>CCR1 for a 2 ms pulse, <c>SERVO_MAX</c></cell><cell></cell></row>
                        </tabular>
                    </statement>
                </task>
                
            </activity>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day15-fill-in">
                <p>The system clock runs at 12 MHz; a prescaler of 60 slows it to
                200 kHz; ARR + 1 = 4000 = <c>PWM_TIMER_MAX</c>.  At 200 kHz one count is
                5 µs, so 1 ms is 200 counts = <c>SERVO_MIN</c>, 1.5 ms is 300 =
                <c>SERVO_MID</c>, and 2 ms is 400 = <c>SERVO_MAX</c>.  Important to know
                <c>SERVO_MIN</c> and <c>SERVO_MAX</c>, since you HAVE to stay between
                those to not damage the servo.</p>
                </instructor>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 4: 10 min = 3 (the files and the driver's two changes) + 1 (TIM16) + 2 (the map's one C fact) + 4 (map commit and reveal) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day15-program">
            <title>Part 4: Reading the Program</title>
            <p>
                The program is <c>Day15_servo_template.c</c>, and it comes with a timer
                driver in two files, <c>tim.c</c> and <c>tim.h</c>.  It also uses your
                own ADC driver, the <c>adc.c</c> and <c>adc.h</c> you built in Lab 5.
                The template is complete except for the five <c>#define</c> lines whose values you just chose and one line in the loop, the map from the potentiometer's reading to a pulse width.  Here is the header, <c>tim.h</c>, and the three functions it declares:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#ifndef TIM_H_
#define TIM_H_

#include <stdint.h>

void tim16_ms_interrupt_init(int milliseconds);
void tim14_pa7_pwm_init(uint16_t prescaleFactor, uint16_t timerMax);
void tim14_pwm_set(uint16_t pulse);

#endif /* TIM_H_ */
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                The driver is the one we used on Day 11x
                (<xref ref="subsec-day11x-driver"/>), with two changes.  The prescale
                factor and the counter's top value are now parameters, passed in from the
                program, rather than <c>#define</c> lines at the top of the driver.  And
                the range check on the value moved.  On Day 11x <c>tim14_pwm_set()</c>
                checked that the value it was given was within the range the counter can
                represent before writing it to <c>CCR1</c>; here it writes what it is
                given, and the range check is in the program, in <c>updateServo()</c>,
                where the range is <c>SERVO_MIN</c> to <c>SERVO_MAX</c>.  Every register
                write in the initialization is the same as before, so open <c>tim.c</c>
                beside <xref ref="fig-tim14-register-map"/> if you want to check.  The
                whole file is printed in <xref ref="subsec-servo-ref-driver"/>.  Here are
                the lines of the initialization that take the two numbers, and the whole
                of <c>tim14_pwm_set()</c>.  The initialization writes
                <c>ARR = timerMax-1</c>, so <c>PWM_TIMER_MAX</c> is the number of counts
                in one period, and <c>tim14_pwm_set()</c> writes <c>CCR1 = value</c>,
                so the value is the number of counts the pin is HIGH, the same convention
                as on Day 11x.  <c>SERVO_MID</c> is the middle of the range; the loop does
                not use it, but it is the number that parks the horn at its center.
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
	// Set prescaler
	TIM14->PSC = prescaleFactor-1;

	// Set autoreload value (counter runs 0..timerMax-1: timerMax counts per period)
	TIM14->ARR = timerMax-1;

	// Set the compare register value - start with a speed of 0
	TIM14->CCR1 = 0;
]]></code></program>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void tim14_pwm_set(uint16_t value) {
	// PWM mode 1: pin is HIGH while CNT < CCR1, i.e. for exactly 'value' counts
	TIM14->CCR1 = value;
}
]]></code></program>
            
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void updateServo(uint16_t value) {
	if (value>SERVO_MAX)
		value = SERVO_MAX;
	else if (value < SERVO_MIN)
		value = SERVO_MIN;
	tim14_pwm_set(value);
}
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                The driver knows the timer, so on Day 11x the limit it applied was the
                timer's.  The program knows the servo, and the servo's safe range, 1 ms to
                2 ms (outside it the servo drives against its own mechanical stops), is
                much narrower than the range the timer can produce, so the program is where
                that limit belongs.
            </p>
            <p>
                The program reads the potentiometer on your breadboard (the knob, not the one inside the servo) twice a second, and TIM14 is busy
                making the pulse, so the timing tick comes from a second timer, TIM16
                (Reference Manual §18, <em>General-purpose timers (TIM16/TIM17)</em>).
                <c>tim16_ms_interrupt_init()</c> is the periodic-interrupt recipe from
                Day 8 (<xref ref="subsec-day8-interrupt-prog"/>) on a different timer, with
                the period as a parameter; the listing below is that recipe line for line.
                TIM16's interrupt is position 21 in the vector table
                (<xref ref="subsec-timers-ref-family"/>), and the startup file's name for
                that slot is <c>TIM16_IRQHandler()</c>.  The handler raises
                <c>timerFlag</c>, and the main loop takes one reading and lowers it
                again.</p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void tim16_ms_interrupt_init(int milliseconds) {
	/* enable clock access to timer 16 (on APB bus) */
	RCC->APBENR2 |= RCC_APBENR2_TIM16EN;

	/* Set prescaler value */
	TIM16->PSC = TIM16_PSC_FACTOR - 1; // starts counting at 0

	/* Set auto-reload value */
	TIM16->ARR = milliseconds - 1;

	/* Clear counter */
	TIM16->CNT = 0;

	/* Enable update interrupt */
	TIM16->DIER |= TIM_DIER_UIE;

	/* Enable timer interrupt in NVIC */
	NVIC_EnableIRQ(TIM16_IRQn);

	/* Enable timer */
	TIM16->CR1 |= TIM_CR1_CEN;
}
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                The last blank is the map from a potentiometer reading, 0 to 4095, onto a
                pulse width between <c>SERVO_MIN</c> and <c>SERVO_MAX</c>.  One C fact to
                keep in mind when you write it: <c>/</c> on two integers discards the
                remainder, so the order in which you multiply and divide matters.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-day15-map">
                <title>Map the potentiometer onto the pulse</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>Write the expression that maps <c>pot_value</c> onto <c>pwm_value</c>,
                    using the names in the template (<c>MAXADC</c> is 4096).  Then check it: what does it give
                    for readings of 0, 2048 and 4095, and are all three inside the
                    servo's safe range?</p>
                </statement>
            </activity>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day15-map">
                <p>The line is
                <c>pwm_value = SERVO_MIN + pot_value*(SERVO_MAX-SERVO_MIN)/MAXADC;</c>.
                A reading of 0 gives 200; 2048 gives 200 + 2048 × 200/4096 = 300; and
                4095 gives 200 + 819,000/4096 = 200 + 199 = 399, so the map never quite
                reaches <c>SERVO_MAX</c>.  That is not a defect; it is what dividing by
                4096 rather than 4095 does, and the top step is one count.</p>
                <p>Two arithmetic notes.  <c>pot_value</c> is an <c>int16_t</c>, and the
                product is computed in <c>int</c>; see <xref ref="subsec-servo-ref-map"/>.
                And <c>updateServo()</c> limits every value the map computes to
                <c>SERVO_MIN</c> through <c>SERVO_MAX</c> before it reaches CCR1, so a slip
                in the map cannot send the servo past its range.  (The initialization is
                the exception: it parks <c>CCR1</c> at 0, so nothing is commanded until the
                first reading.)</p>
            </instructor>
            <p>
                Here is the whole program as you download it.
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
/* servo.c -- Control a servo motor with a potentiometer
 * Potentiometer:  	Analog channel PA0
 * Servo PWM input:	PA7
 */

#include <stdio.h>
#include "ES28.h"
#include "uart.h"
#include "adc.h"
#include "tim.h"

/* Clock runs at 12MHz */
#define PWM_PSC_FACTOR			// prescaler
#define PWM_TIMER_MAX			// 20 ms
#define SERVO_MIN 				// 1 ms
#define SERVO_MID				// 1.5 ms
#define SERVO_MAX				// 2 ms

#define MAXADC 		4096		// 12-bit converter

volatile uint8_t timerFlag = 0;		// Do not forget the volatile!

void updateServo(uint16_t value);

// Main program
int main(void) {
    uint16_t pot_value = 0;
  uint16_t pwm_value = SERVO_MIN;

  uart2_init();
  tim14_pa7_pwm_init(PWM_PSC_FACTOR, PWM_TIMER_MAX);
  pa0_adc_init();

  printf("Potentiometer-controlled servo\n\r");

  timerFlag = 0;
  tim16_ms_interrupt_init(500);

  while(1) {
 	if(timerFlag==1) {
	    start_conversion();
	    pot_value = adc_read();	 // Pot value (0 to MAXADC-1)

/*******************/
		pwm_value =	     		// Map pot_value to pwm_value
/*******************/

		updateServo(pwm_value);		// Update the PWM
		printf("pot_value=%d, \tpwm_value=%d\n\r", pot_value, pwm_value);
 		timerFlag=0;					// Put the flag down
	}
	// Processor could sleep here
  }
  return 0;                            // never reached
}
void updateServo(uint16_t value) {
	if (value>SERVO_MAX)
		value = SERVO_MAX;
	else if (value < SERVO_MIN)
		value = SERVO_MIN;
	tim14_pwm_set(value);
}

void TIM16_IRQHandler(void) {
	__disable_irq();
	/* Clear UIF in status register */
	TIM16->SR = ~TIM_SR_UIF;
	timerFlag = 1;
	__enable_irq();
}
]]></code></program>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 5: 51 min = 5 (project) + 8 (fill the blanks) + 4 (wire) + 5 (predict) + 24 (build, run, capture; checkpoint at about minute 78, mid-beat) + 5 (center the knob, finish demos) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day15-build">
            <title>Part 5: Design Exercise, Verifying the Pulse on the Oscilloscope</title>
            <p>
                Now we'll build it.  We'll verify the pulse on the oscilloscope before the
                servo is ever attached: that is your safety check, so that you won't break
                the servo.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-day15-build">
                <title>A potentiometer-controlled pulse on PA7</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>Work through these in order.  The servo stays unplugged until tomorrow.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day15-build-project">
                    <statement><p>Copy your <c>TemplateProject</c> to a new project
                    named <c>Servo</c>.  Download <c>Day15_servo_template.c</c>,
                    <c>tim.c</c> and <c>tim.h</c> from Canvas; the two <c>.c</c> files
                    go into <c>Src</c> and <c>tim.h</c> into <c>Inc</c>.  You'll also
                    need your ADC code.  If you have previously added your <c>adc.c</c>
                    and <c>adc.h</c> from Lab 5 to the <c>mylib</c> folder you are good
                    to go; otherwise copy <c>adc.c</c> into <c>Src</c> and <c>adc.h</c>
                    into <c>Inc</c>.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day15-build-blanks">
                    <statement><p>Complete the five <c>#define</c> lines with the numbers
                    you chose earlier today (the prescaler factor, the counter's top value,
                    and the three compare values for a 1 ms, a 1.5 ms and a 2 ms pulse) and
                    the map line.  If your ADC functions have different names from
                    <c>pa0_adc_init()</c>, <c>start_conversion()</c> and <c>adc_read()</c>,
                    adjust those three calls to your own names.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day15-build-wire">
                    <statement><p>Wire the potentiometer to A0 (PA0) exactly as in the ADC
                    chapter (<xref ref="fig-adc-test-circuit"/>): the outer terminals to
                    3.3 V and GND, the wiper to A0.  Connect the AD2's channel 1, the
                    orange lead, to PA7 (D11), and channel 1's ground lead, the one with
                    the white stripe, to a GND pin.  Nothing else is connected to
                    PA7.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day15-build-predict">
                    <statement><p>Before you capture anything, write down three
                    things: the time base that shows one full 20 ms period on the
                    screen; the trigger you will use; and the <c>pwm_value</c> your
                    program will print with the knob at one end, in the middle, and at
                    the other end.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day15-build-run">
                    <statement><p>Build, run, and open a terminal.  Turn the knob from
                    one end to the other: <c>pot_value</c> should run from 0 to 4095,
                    and <c>pwm_value</c> between the numbers you predicted.  On the
                    oscilloscope, trigger on the rising edge and measure the pulse
                    width and the period at each end of the knob.
                    Check what you measure against the prediction you wrote down.
                    <xref ref="fig-servo-scope-1ms"/> and <xref ref="fig-servo-scope-2ms"/>
                    show the time base and the trigger a good capture uses.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day15-build-center">
                    <statement><p>Find the knob position that gives a 1.5 ms pulse.  Remove and
                    reattach the knob, if necessary, so that it is at the midway
                    position.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <figure xml:id="fig-servo-scope-1ms">
                <caption>The pulse on PA7 with the knob at one end, captured on the AD2 at 5 ms/div with a rising-edge trigger on channel 1 (the empty lower half of the screen is cropped out).  The cursors
                measure the pulse width at 1.0056 ms and the period at 19.91 ms, which
                the cursor table reports as 50.2 Hz.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day15-Servos/fig-servo-scope-1ms.png" width="100%"/>
            </figure>
            <figure xml:id="fig-servo-scope-2ms">
                <caption>The same capture with the knob at the other end.  The pulse is now 2.008 ms wide, and the period is unchanged at 19.93 ms,
                still 20 ms to within the cursors' placement.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day15-Servos/fig-servo-scope-2ms.png" width="100%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                <term>Nothing on the screen, or not what you predicted?</term>  Work down
                this list, since each symptom points somewhere different.
            </p>
            <ol>
                <li><term>The project will not build.</term>  Look at the <c>#define</c>
                lines first: a blank one puts nothing where the compiler expects a number,
                and the error is reported at the line that uses the name rather than at the
                blank itself.  Then check that <c>tim.c</c> and your <c>adc.c</c> are in the
                project, and that there is exactly one <c>main()</c> in it.</li>
                <li><term>No pulse at all.</term>  The fault is in the wiring or the build.
                PA7 is D11, and channel 1's ground lead has to be on a GND pin.</li>
                <li><term>A pulse of the wrong width or period.</term>  One of the
                <c>#define</c> values is off.  <c>PWM_PSC_FACTOR</c> and
                <c>PWM_TIMER_MAX</c> set the period; <c>SERVO_MIN</c> and <c>SERVO_MAX</c>
                set the width at the two ends of the knob.</li>
                <li><term>A pulse that does not move with the knob.</term>  The fault is in
                the ADC or the map.  Look at <c>pot_value</c> in the printout: if it is not
                changing, the fault is before the map; if it is changing and
                <c>pwm_value</c> is not, the fault is in the map.</li>
            </ol>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day15-template-complete">
                <p>The completed template.  It differs from the download in exactly two places: the five <c>#define</c> values and the map line.</p>
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
/* Clock runs at 12MHz */
#define PWM_PSC_FACTOR	60			// prescaler: 12 MHz / 60 = 200 kHz
#define PWM_TIMER_MAX	4000		// 20 ms: 200 kHz / 4000 = 50 Hz
#define SERVO_MIN 		200			// 1 ms
#define SERVO_MID		300			// 1.5 ms
#define SERVO_MAX		400			// 2 ms

#define MAXADC 		4096		// 12-bit converter

	...

	    start_conversion();
	    pot_value = adc_read();	 // Pot value (0 to MAXADC-1)

		pwm_value = SERVO_MIN + pot_value*(SERVO_MAX-SERVO_MIN)/MAXADC;

		updateServo(pwm_value);		// Update the PWM
]]></code></program>
                <p>Have each student demonstrate the sweeping pulse and say, without looking
                at their table, why they picked their prescaler and what CCR1 value
                <c>SERVO_MID</c> produces.  A student may go on to the stretch as soon as their own capture matches the reference captures.  The time-base answer: 5 ms/div puts a
                whole period on the screen (her captures); 1 ms/div shows the pulse
                but not the period.  Trigger: rising edge on channel 1.</p>
            </instructor>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day15-checkpoint">
                <p>This exercise takes students a LONG time; lots of troubleshooting
                to do.  Checkpoint at about minute 78, mid-exercise.  Triage in this
                order, by symptom:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>No pulse at all: wiring or build.  PA7 is D11; the AD2's ground
                    lead is on a GND pin; the build picked up <c>tim.c</c> and
                    <c>adc.c</c>; exactly one <c>main()</c> in the project.</li>
                    <li>A pulse of the wrong width or period: a <c>#define</c>.
                    <c>PWM_PSC_FACTOR</c> and <c>PWM_TIMER_MAX</c> set the period;
                    <c>SERVO_MIN</c> and <c>SERVO_MAX</c> set the width at the two
                    ends.</li>
                    <li>A pulse that does not move with the knob: the ADC or the map.
                    Is <c>pot_value</c> changing in the printout?  If not, the wiper is
                    not on A0 or the ADC calls are not their own.  If it is and
                    <c>pwm_value</c> is stuck at <c>SERVO_MIN</c>, the map divides
                    before it multiplies.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>The x-hour is the overflow by design: a student still here at the
                end of class finishes tomorrow, before wiring the servo.  Part 6's first
                beat and the close still fit at minute 100; the wiring itself is
                Wednesday's.  Point a stuck student at the symptom list in the text first;
                the ladder above is the part that needs you.</p>
            </instructor>

            <activity xml:id="act-day15-stretch">
                <title>Going further</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>These are optional, for when your pulse is verified.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day15-stretch-millis">
                    <statement><p>Replace the TIM16 interrupt with
                    <c>milliseconds()</c> from Day 12
                    (<xref ref="subsec-day12-main-loop"/>): rewrite the loop so that
                    the sampling interval is a <c>#define</c> and no interrupt of your own is needed.  Nothing else about the program changes.  In Lab 8 you are
                    asked to keep track of the sampling interval this way.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day15-stretch-finer">
                    <statement><p>The map turns 4096 potentiometer readings into 200 timer
                    counts.  How many readings share one timer count, and what does that
                    mean for how the knob feels as you turn it?  Then: if you wanted one
                    timer count per reading, what would <c>PWM_TIMER_MAX</c> have to be, and
                    what would go wrong with the pulse period?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day15-stretch">
                <p><term>The millisecond tick.</term>  Keep the time of the last
                reading and compare against it, the way the Day 12 main loop does:</p>
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#define SAMPLE_MS	500

  uint32_t last_sample = milliseconds();

  while(1) {
	if (milliseconds() - last_sample >= SAMPLE_MS) {
		last_sample = milliseconds();
		start_conversion();
		pot_value = adc_read();
		pwm_value = SERVO_MIN + pot_value*(SERVO_MAX-SERVO_MIN)/MAXADC;
		updateServo(pwm_value);
		printf("pot_value=%d, \tpwm_value=%d\n\r", pot_value, pwm_value);
	}
  }
]]></code></program>
                <p>The <c>tim16_ms_interrupt_init(500)</c> call, the flag and the
                handler come out.  This is the shape Lab 8 asks for, with
                <c>T</c> as the <c>#define</c>.</p>
                <p><term>One count per reading.</term>  4096/200 is about 20 readings to a
                count, so roughly 20 codes of knob travel go by before the horn moves, which
                is what a student who turns the knob slowly and watches <c>pwm_value</c>
                will notice.  For one count per reading you would need 4096 counts across
                the 1 ms window, so <m>T_0</m> = 244 ns and ARR + 1 for a 20 ms period would
                be 81,920, which a 16-bit register cannot hold.  And 244 ns is far below the
                servo's 1 µs dead band, so the servo could not act on a single step even if
                the register could.</p>
            </instructor>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 6: 7 min = 4 (the why, the regulator, the drawing) + 3 (the leads and the order) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day15-power">
            <title>Part 6: Powering the Servo</title>
            <p>
                The reading said the servo is powered from a separate supply and never from
                the Nucleo.  Here is the reason: the servo may draw about
                250 mA while moving, and much more if it is stalled, while your laptop's
                USB port supplies at most 500 mA for the whole board and everything on it.
                High current draw by the servo can starve the Nucleo, causing it to brown
                out and reset itself.  A GPIO pin is worse still: the STM32C031C6 datasheet
                rates any single I/O pin for at most 20 mA, so a servo's power lead on a
                pin would exceed that rating many times over.  So we power it from the same regulator board we used
                for the motor (<xref ref="fig-tb6612-regulator"/>), and
                <xref ref="fig-servo-powering"/> shows how the servo connects to it.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-servo-powering">
                <caption>How the servo is powered.  The servo's red lead, the middle one,
                goes to the regulator board's 5V pin.  Its brown lead goes to the ground
                rail, which the Nucleo's GND and the board's GND pin share, and its orange
                lead carries the pulse from D11 (PA7).  The Nucleo's 3.3 V and GND feed
                the breadboard's rails.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day15-Servos/towerProPowering.png" width="95%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                On the SG92R in your kit the three leads are brown, red and orange (<xref ref="fig-servo-leads"/>), and <xref ref="fig-servo-powering"/> shows
                where each one goes.  Unplug everything, the USB cable and the adapter,
                before you make any change to the wiring.  After the wiring has been
                checked, the order is the one from the motor chapter: USB first, for the
                Nucleo, then the adapter.  We'll do the wiring tomorrow.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-servo-leads">
                <caption>The SG92R on its stand, with the solar tracker's arm bolted to
                its horn.  The three leads leave at the right.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day15-Servos/slide09_a204d4b1.jpg" width="70%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                Tomorrow we'll connect the servo to its 5 V and watch it follow the
                potentiometer.  On Thursday we'll replace the potentiometer with two photocells and start
                on the solar tracker.  Thursday's reading is about the photocell.
            </p>
            <!-- Close, 5 min.  Presenter note for the deck (delivery 2): bring the
                 servo, the regulator board and its adapter, and your multimeter
                 tomorrow.  No homework is due Thursday (Petra, 2026-09-03). -->
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- Day 15x In-Class: the session after Petra's pass on Day 15 (plans/day15x.md).
         OWES: sec-servo-day15x with Part 1 (recall of the leads and the power rule,
         pointer only), Part 2 (universal re-verification of the pulse, then the servo
         on the regulator's 5 V following the pot: fig-servo-pot-wiring from
         assets/images/Day15-Servos/towerProPot.png, pot on A0), Part 3 (the
         two-channel ADC read as Lab 5 recall), the three-branch checkpoint ladder in
         an <instructor>, and the close with Thursday's bring-list as a presenter note.
         The chapter introduction's "on the second we'll connect the servo to its own
         5 V supply" is a promise this section has yet to keep. -->

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-servo-reference">
        <title>Reference: The Servo Command, the Timer Arithmetic, and the Driver</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Lookup material for the servo.  Nothing here is new; it is the same
                information arranged for a quick look-up.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-servo-ref-command">
            <title>The Command Pulse</title>
            <p>
                A hobby servo is commanded by a pulse repeated about every 20 ms
                (50 Hz).  The width of the HIGH pulse is the position command, and the
                servo holds the position only as long as the pulses keep coming; with
                no signal there is no power on the motor.  The convention:
            </p>
            <tabular halign="left">
                <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Pulse width</cell>
                    <cell>Position</cell>
                </row>
                <row><cell>1 ms</cell><cell>−90°, one end of the travel</cell></row>
                <row><cell>1.5 ms</cell><cell>0°, the middle</cell></row>
                <row><cell>2 ms</cell><cell>+90°, the other end of the travel</cell></row>
            </tabular>
            <p>
                The endpoints are the convention, and a given servo does not
                necessarily reach them; the SG90 datasheet says approximately 180° of
                rotation.  Pulse widths outside 1 ms to 2 ms can drive the servo against
                its own stops, which is why the program limits every value it computes to
                <c>SERVO_MIN</c> through <c>SERVO_MAX</c> before writing <c>CCR1</c>.  The
                initialization is the one exception: <c>tim14_pa7_pwm_init()</c> parks
                <c>CCR1</c> at 0, so until the first potentiometer reading arrives there
                is no pulse at all and the servo is not commanded anywhere.
                The SG92R's dead band width is 1 µs: a change in pulse width smaller
                than that has no effect.  Its other specs, from the
                <url href="external/datasheets/C17481_SG92R_datasheet.pdf">datasheet</url>:
                stall torque 2.5 kgf-cm at 4.8 V, operating speed 0.1 s per 60°, operating
                voltage 4.8 V, POM and carbon-fiber gears.  It draws about 250 mA while
                moving, and much more when it is stalled.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-servo-ref-timer">
            <title>The Timer Arithmetic</title>
            <p>
                For a pulse period <m>T_p</m>, the counter divides each period into
                ARR + 1 counts, so the smallest pulse width, and the smallest change in
                pulse width, is <m>T_0 = T_p/(\text{ARR}+1)</m>.  The prescaler and the
                auto-reload value have to satisfy
                <m>\text{prescaleFactor} \times (\text{ARR}+1) = 12\text{ MHz} \times T_p</m>, with
                ARR at most 65,535.  The number of steps between 1 ms and 2 ms is
                <m>1\text{ ms}/T_0</m>, the servo's resolution counted in steps rather than
                in microseconds (<xref ref="subsec-day11x-resolution"/> measures it the
                other way), and the nominal angle per step is 180° divided by that number.  A step finer than the servo's dead band, or than its gear train's backlash,
                is more finely grained than is worth doing.  What is written into the registers is each
                number minus one, because the prescaler and the counter both start
                counting at 0: <c>TIM14->PSC = prescaleFactor-1</c> and
                <c>TIM14->ARR = timerMax-1</c>.  In PWM mode 1 the pin is HIGH while
                the counter is below <c>CCR1</c>, so <c>CCR1</c> is the number of
                counts the pin is HIGH.
            </p>
            <p>
                A program that needs a periodic tick while TIM14 is busy making a PWM
                signal uses a second timer.  TIM16 (Reference Manual §18,
                <em>General-purpose timers (TIM16/TIM17)</em>) has the same prescaler,
                counter and auto-reload core, its interrupt is position 21 in the
                vector table, and its handler is <c>TIM16_IRQHandler()</c>.  The initialization is the one from Day 8 on a different timer, with the
                period as a parameter.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-servo-ref-driver">
            <title>The Driver: tim.c</title>
            <p>
                The whole of <c>tim.c</c>, with its comments shortened.  <c>tim14_pa7_pwm_init()</c> is the Day 11x
                driver with the two timer numbers as parameters, and its register
                writes are explained line by line in <xref ref="subsec-day11x-driver"/>.
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#include "tim.h"
#include "ES28.h"

// Clock runs at 12MHz
#define TIM16_PSC_FACTOR	12000		// 12 MHz / 12000 = 1 kHz

void tim16_ms_interrupt_init(int milliseconds) {
	RCC->APBENR2 |= RCC_APBENR2_TIM16EN;	// clock access to timer 16
	TIM16->PSC = TIM16_PSC_FACTOR - 1;		// starts counting at 0
	TIM16->ARR = milliseconds - 1;
	TIM16->CNT = 0;
	TIM16->DIER |= TIM_DIER_UIE;			// update interrupt
	NVIC_EnableIRQ(TIM16_IRQn);
	TIM16->CR1 |= TIM_CR1_CEN;
}

void tim14_pwm_set(uint16_t value) {
	// PWM mode 1: pin is HIGH while CNT < CCR1, i.e. for exactly 'value' counts
	TIM14->CCR1 = value;
}

void tim14_pa7_pwm_init(uint16_t prescaleFactor, uint16_t timerMax) {
	// Enable clock access to GPIOA
	RCC->IOPENR |=  RCC_IOPENR_GPIOAEN;

	// Set PA7 in alternate function mode (10)
	GPIOA->MODER &= ~GPIO_MODER_MODE7_Msk;
	GPIOA->MODER |= GPIO_ALTERNATE << GPIO_MODER_MODE7_Pos;

	// Set PA7 alternate function type (AF4, 0100)
	GPIOA->AFR[0] &= ~GPIO_AFRL_AFSEL7_Msk;
	GPIOA->AFR[0] |= GPIO_AF4 << GPIO_AFRL_AFSEL7_Pos;

	// Enable clock access to Timer 14
	RCC->APBENR2 |= RCC_APBENR2_TIM14EN;

	// Set prescaler
	TIM14->PSC = prescaleFactor-1;

	// Set autoreload value (counter runs 0..timerMax-1: timerMax counts per period)
	TIM14->ARR = timerMax-1;

	// Set the compare register value - start with a speed of 0
	TIM14->CCR1 = 0;

	// Set pwm mode 1 (0110): pin is high up to capture value, then goes low
	TIM14->CCMR1 &= ~TIM_CCMR1_OC1M_Msk;
	TIM14->CCMR1 |= TIM_OC1_PWM1 << TIM_CCMR1_OC1M_Pos;

	// Make sure timer is in output mode; this is default value
	TIM14->CCMR1 &= ~TIM_CCMR1_CC1S_Msk;
	TIM14->CCMR1 |= TIM_CC1_OUTPUT << TIM_CCMR1_CC1S_Pos;

	// Enable TIM14_CH1 in output mode
	TIM14->CCER |= TIM_CCER_CC1E;

	// Generate an update event - this clears counter, prescaler counter, updates registers
	TIM14->EGR |= TIM_EGR_UG;

	// Enable timer
	TIM14->CR1 |= TIM_CR1_CEN;
}
]]></code></program>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-servo-ref-map">
            <title>Scaling One Integer Range onto Another</title>
            <p>
                Two C facts matter whenever one integer range is scaled onto another.
                First, <c>/</c> on two integers discards the remainder, so a scale factor
                smaller than 1 written as a division comes out as 0; multiply before you
                divide.  Second, the product is computed in <c>int</c>, which is 32 bits
                on the STM32C031C6, so a 12-bit reading times a range of a few hundred
                fits easily (4095 × 200 = 819,000 is more than a 16-bit variable holds,
                and fits in an <c>int</c> with room to spare).  And if the divisor is the
                number of possible readings, 4096, rather than the largest reading, 4095,
                the top of the output range is one step short, which is usually fine and
                is worth knowing.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

</chapter>
